TELOMERE-CCC2-1:{"chunk":"c04","format":"ccc2-wire/1","records":[{"buys":"the same generative move — squares manufacture positivity; the prime sum −2ΣΛ(n)n^{−1/2}g(log n) is a weighted vertex-tower sum, our native objects carrying Weil's functional","checksum":"b244193a4258c3c0335175778fcf4f4a479b7a11b926a9196a4f51f8a7043503","id":"B1","limits":"dictionary is translation, not proof; the positivity must come from the geometry independently or the argument is circular","type":"B","x":"Weil's square h = |H|²","y":"our autocorrelation square P(θ) = |Q(e^{iθ})|² (D9)"},{"checksum":"42dae1cc2080ed388d40a64cdba3f9a5c22824e2f404bf9caf2542fcda2a1684","id":"C1","kind":"claim","label":"Positivity is blind left of the line","note":"Dirichlet-series positivity Σ_nΛ(n)n^{−σ}P(t log n) ≥ 0 converges only for σ > 1. Everything left of Re(s) = 1 comes from analytic input: the zero's own partial-fraction term 1/(s−ρ) and bounds on ζ′/ζ. Any argument using only (Λ ≥ 0 plus the pole at s = 1) can at most recover ζ(1+it) ≠ 0 — the inputs do not even define the objects left of the line. Honest division of labor: geometry owns the positivity inputs, analysis owns the continuation and bounds.","status":"exact","type":"C"},{"checksum":"723ba27c7c284ca31b4e6e6c2b1f982fb2b9247c51cb11e6e08453abffee02f5","id":"C2","kind":"claim","label":"The parity barrier (Selberg)","note":"Pure sieve/combinatorial positivity cannot resolve the parity of ω(n) — so some oscillatory/analytic input is necessary, not optional. Classical cited theorem (Selberg), not re-derived in text; 9c-via-pure-positivity was blocked by theorem, not by lack of trying.","status":"conjectural","type":"C"},{"checksum":"3f9c4cd8d75eb1cde2fe7d41bc2b20e7c23f3b329d42567cd17c61d146a6a736","id":"C3","kind":"claim","label":"The literature has saturated the polynomial optimization","note":"Cited literature, not verified in text: Mossinghoff–Trudgian–Yang (2022, arXiv:2212.06867): σ ≥ 1−1/(5.558691 log|t|) via a degree-16 optimized nonnegative trig polynomial — 'the trigonometric-polynomial method is near its intrinsic limit.' Bellotti–Trudgian–Yang (2026): 1/(4.896 log|t|). 'Better polynomials from geometry' as an improvement avenue is essentially closed, by the experts' own assessment.","status":"conjectural","type":"C"},{"checksum":"5f6cbfbc726a65263f5eb389729f3486446a74e8915a99def39393af4baea854","id":"C4","kind":"claim","label":"Zeros as regulators of the face-sum error","note":"Exact translation of D1: E(x) = ψ(x) − x is made of zeros; the face-sum knows the primes, the zeros know the face-sum's error. Programmatic thesis toward Weil (heuristic): Weil's positivity W(f∗f̃) ≥ 0 ⟺ RH, and via the explicit formula W of a test function is a weighted integral of E(x) plus archimedean terms — so Weil's positivity becomes a positivity constraint on the incidence complex's face-sum error. That is the span's shape.","status":"exact","type":"C"},{"checksum":"c640a15ce57344b684b4879cedd98aa2aebde2e1462b8a17bab1d0ee82147eb8","id":"C5","kind":"question","label":"10a-step3 forward pointer: optimize over H","note":"Which square test function maximizes the positivity's constraining power? The Weil analogue of the degree-16 polynomial optimization (D9).","status":"unstated","type":"C"},{"checksum":"145acf3e965a8377d702aa32ae3eece5108720fd46880790647a10b9370d92fb","id":"C6","kind":"question","label":"9c′ open computable question","note":"Do the saturated degree-16 optimizers have small-support autocorrelation structure? Unknown — the literature's are black-box (simulated annealing/SDP).","status":"unstated","type":"C"},{"checksum":"85a13acc866af8c487a9262bf784e8c579b57cb0272e4d87cd2a485fe08b45ce","generates":[{"prescription":"choose q on residues (e.g. q=(1,2,1), q=(1,1,1), q=(1,0,1), q=(1,3,3,1))","stage":"charge pattern"},{"prescription":"form a_d = 2Σ_j q_j q_{j+d} and the trig polynomial P(θ) = |Q(e^{iθ})|² ≥ 0","stage":"autocorrelation"},{"prescription":"read a₁/a₀; a₁ > a₀ forces the line-zero multiplicity m < 1 via a₀ − a₁m ≥ 0","stage":"exclusion test"}],"honest_boundary":"The family explains the positivity inputs the literature optimizes; it does NOT improve them — 'better polynomials from geometry' is closed as an improvement avenue (C3). Do not present this as a stronger zero detector.","honest_label":"exact","id":"D1","note":"via autocorrelation of a charge pattern q on residues: the family the literature optimizes over gets a residue-pattern interpretation — the sieve explains the positivity inputs even where it cannot improve them.","rule":"P(θ) = |Q(e^{iθ})|² ≥ 0,  a_d = 2Σ_j q_j q_{j+d}  (d ≥ 1);  a₁ > a₀ kills simple line zeros (from a₀ − a₁m ≥ 0 forcing m < 1).","subtitle":"D9 viable core — the sieve produces positivity inputs geometrically (autocorrelation family)","type":"D","verified":[{"check":"q=(1,1) → 2+2cosθ","expected":"a₁/a₀ = 1, too weak (the classical fact)"},{"check":"q=(1,2,1) → 6+8cosθ+2cos2θ","expected":"= 2×(3-4-1), excludes"},{"check":"q=(1,1,1) → 3+4cosθ+2cos2θ","expected":"a₁/a₀ = 4/3, excludes — the unweighted 3-block works"},{"check":"q=(1,0,1) (twin residues {0,2})","expected":"a₁ = 0, blind"},{"check":"q=(1,3,3,1)","expected":"a₁/a₀ = 3/2, stronger"}]},{"checksum":"7fbae14ffbdb92ae90a4d36bdf054c987b19dc6bffa403e67d219e6094e20965","generates":[{"prescription":"compute ψ(x) as the prime-power sum — exactly the D1 face-sum, no independent ψ routine","stage":"prime side from sieve"},{"prescription":"truncate S_T(x) = x − 2Re Σ_{0<γ≤T} x^ρ/ρ − log(2π) − ½log(1−x^{−2}) (zeros from mpmath, first 400, γ ≤ 679.7)","stage":"zero side"},{"prescription":"take max |ψ − S_T| over x ∈ [20.5, 200.5] (half-integers avoid jumps) at T = 50, 100, 200, 400 and check decrease inside the O(x log²x/T) truncation bound","stage":"compare"}],"honest_boundary":"The explicit formula itself is cited (Davenport), not derived; zeros are mpmath-computed to γ ≤ 679.7; truncation error is controlled by the classical bound, not eliminated.","honest_label":"computationally_verified","id":"D2","note":"The error term E(x) is made of zeros: D4's 'beachhead #2' concrete — the face-sum knows the primes; the zeros know the face-sum's error; this is the joint both abutments share.","rule":"ψ(x) = x − Σ_ρ x^ρ/ρ − log(2π) − ½log(1−x^{−2})  (x > 1, not a prime power; classical exact theorem, Davenport);  E(x) = ψ(x) − x = −Σ_ρ x^ρ/ρ − log(2π) − …","subtitle":"D10a-step1 — the explicit formula, grounded in sieve objects","type":"D","verified":[{"check":"max |ψ − S_T| over x ∈ [20.5, 200.5]","expected":"T=50 → 4.55; T=100 → 2.46; T=200 → 1.95; T=400 → 1.36, decreasing in T, inside O(x log²x/T)"},{"check":"pointwise oscillation at x = 100.5","expected":"ψ−x = −6.4547 vs zero-sum −6.5852"}]},{"checksum":"941e930bdad3976cd17d8a1162c29a09540f763e975813e72cc7e9ec1ed7c88f","generates":[{"prescription":"pick h_a(r) = e^{−ar²} = |e^{−ar²/2}|² and form its exact Fourier partner g(u) = (1/2π)∫h(r)e^{−iru}dr","stage":"square test function"},{"prescription":"evaluate W(h) from prime powers + digamma integral + pole terms only — no zeros used","stage":"prime-side evaluation"},{"prescription":"compare against the zero-side sum Σ_ρ h(γ_ρ) at a = 0.02, 0.10, 0.50 and check W(h_a) ≥ 0 with no zeros in the computation","stage":"agreement check"}],"honest_boundary":"Weil's criterion — W(h) ≥ 0 for all admissible square-type h ⟺ RH (Weil 1952; Bombieri 2000 exposition) — is cited, not derived. What we derived: the prime-side presentation, its cancellatory structure, and the square dictionary. Translation ≠ proof; the positivity must come from the geometry independently or the argument is circular.","honest_label":"computationally_verified","id":"D3","note":"via the square generative move: prime-side positivity is not termwise but a global cancellation — archimedean background negative, nearly canceling the pole, the prime-power sum eating the remainder; on the zero side termwise, on the prime side a balance; the bridge is the formula equating them.","rule":"Symmetric Guinand–Weil: Σ_ρ h(γ_ρ) = h(i/2)+h(−i/2) + (1/2π)∫h(r)[Re dig(1/4+ir/2)−logπ]dr − 2Σ_{n≥2} Λ(n)n^{−1/2}g(log n);  test family h_a(r) = e^{−ar²} = |e^{−ar²/2}|² — a square.","subtitle":"D10a-step2 — Weil's formula as prime-side positivity","type":"D","verified":[{"check":"zero side vs prime side (60 zeros, 26152 prime powers)","expected":"a=0.02: |diff| 2.4e-16; a=0.10: 2.2e-17; a=0.50: 5.3e-15 — machine-precision agreement, incl. the delicate a=0.5 cancellation (pole+arch−primesum = 0 to 15 digits)"},{"check":"positivity with no zeros used","expected":"W(h_a) ≥ 0 in all cases"},{"check":"cancellation components at a=0.5","expected":"pole 2.266297 + archimedean (−1.196347) − prime drain 1.069950 ≈ 0"},{"check":"bug audit","expected":"first run grouped prime powers by prime with an early break, silently dropping p ≥ 3 contributions; caught by the a=0.1 mismatch, fixed by sorting + full summation — verification from the corrected run"}]},{"checksum":"7d6167f8dc493ce5bff871f44fc95012d1d150e0b04e2b4e1823ef360c57da2d","id":"G1","result":"(a) ζ_R is an Euler product ⟹ zero-free for Re(s) > 1 — exact, since |R_p|p^{−σ} < 1 means no factor vanishes; the zero-exclusion machine runs with nothing to exclude. (b) The pole only gets heavier: F(σ) ~ 2/(σ−1) (verified with tail correction: 19.33 vs 20.00 at σ = 1.1), making the inequality easier to satisfy, not stronger. Min S = 3F(σ)+4ReF(σ+it)+ReF(σ+2it) > 0 verified on a t-grid (deformation_kill.py).","title":"D9: the D8 deformation seed killed","transfer":"The speculation was ours and the evidence killed it: never re-run 'sieve deformations parametrize new positivity for constraining zeros' — the exclusion inputs only bite where zeros can exist.","tried":"twin deformation ζ_R(s) = ∏_p(1−|R_p|p^{−s})^{−1}, F = −ζ_R′/ζ_R = Σ_p|R_p|(log p)/(p^s−|R_p|) with coefficients ≥ 0, so the 3-4-1 machine transports","type":"G","verdict":"Deformations repackage the pole; they create no new zero-detector."},{"checksum":"6bc0992f1c7cf63c1955f1c853a573313726c3f226314f87731032d92ad09f8d","from":"D1","id":"L1","rel":"supports","to":"D2","type":"L"},{"checksum":"de74fd18980e9238bb5cb2136b16032ef3ceec52cbee56c2961d9d292eb8ff7e","from":"G1","id":"L2","rel":"supports","to":"V1","type":"L"},{"checksum":"091245d435bda003328205549eb31aedcef083c406a5edf5992e5c71895fbe81","from":"C1","id":"L3","rel":"supports","to":"V1","type":"L"},{"checksum":"aac61e5fd1fa0e1f58ca4e529251d24b0cc2b6fdcd74024c7ce6c032ada33d4b","from":"C2","id":"L4","rel":"supports","to":"V1","type":"L"},{"checksum":"e5878f1817cb810b6f0ad25a4481a3a82a15c2ce52c149feac0d6ae99c64fd02","from":"C3","id":"L5","rel":"supports","to":"V1","type":"L"},{"checksum":"8c295d1ac2622239cf22ddf629038f7408f82dd8666c7cca5034c7b1962c69b2","from":"B1","id":"L6","rel":"defines","to":"D3","type":"L"},{"checksum":"f43dca315f53fcd8766fdc7686a310e0496d99f61a089799ffc37986bc4b7abb","consequence":"Closed by principled limits (G1)+(C1)+(C2)+(C3), not difficulty. What remains: 9c′ (the explanatory autocorrelation program, C6) and the hybrid division of labor as the bridge's true shape — the 'almost' is now a measured almost: we know exactly how far the bridge goes, what it carries, and what must carry the rest.","id":"V1","status":"killed","subject":"9c-as-'prove RH by stronger positivity'","type":"V"}],"registry":{"B1":"B","C1":"C","C2":"C","C3":"C","C4":"C","C5":"C","C6":"C","D1":"D","D2":"D","D3":"D","G1":"G","L1":"L","L2":"L","L3":"L","L4":"L","L5":"L","L6":"L","V1":"V"},"wire_checksum":"d27e25d204c4d0d3df17c3e4fc10ea7660ed7939a61f27dcd47632d560ab2580"}:TELOMERE-CCC2-1